 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "d1",
   "narration": {
    "en": "Let us put the main ideas in order, one after another."
   },
   "actions": [
    {
     "do": "show",
     "target": "ttl",
     "at": 0.1
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "d2",
   "narration": {
    "en": "An algorithm is a systematic procedure to solve a problem."
   },
   "actions": [
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "b0",
     "at": 0.2
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "t0",
     "at": 0.3
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "q0",
     "at": 0.35
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "d3",
   "narration": {
    "en": "It consists of basic steps that we know how to execute."
   },
   "actions": [
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "a0",
     "at": 0.1
    },
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "b1",
     "at": 0.2
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "t1",
     "at": 0.3
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "q1",
     "at": 0.35
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "d4",
   "narration": {
    "en": "We can repeat a step many times, for example for each j in 1, 2, ..., n, check whether j divides n."
   },
   "actions": [
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "a1",
     "at": 0.1
    },
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "b2",
     "at": 0.2
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "t2",
     "at": 0.3
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "q2",
     "at": 0.35
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "d5",
   "narration": {
    "en": "To list the divisors of 12 we test every j from 1 to 12 and keep those that divide 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12."
   },
   "actions": [
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "b3",
     "at": 0.2
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "t3",
     "at": 0.3
    },
    {
     "do": "write",
     "target": "q3",
     "at": 0.35
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "d6",
   "narration": {
    "en": "We can also count how many steps it takes in terms of the size of the input, and so judge its efficiency."
   },
   "actions": [
    {
     "do": "draw",
     "target": "a3",
     "at": 0.1
